WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:17.340
<v Ira Glass>On a Thursday morning in August, 2015, a staffer at a big hospital in downtown Houston, St. Joseph Medical Center, heard a Code Green on the intercom and did what they always did-- ran over to the room. Then it was a Code Blue. Code Blue means somebody needs immediate medical attention. Same room.
<v Ira Glass>2015年8月一个周四的早上，休斯顿市中心圣约瑟夫医学中心的一名员工在对讲机里听到了“绿色警报”，于是做了他们平时总会做的事——跑向那个房间。接着响起了“蓝色警报”。蓝色警报意味着有人需要立即接受医疗救治。在同一个房间。

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:36.650
<v Scott Shepherd>And I just see blood everywhere, all over the floor. I was actually shocked. It was just kind of smeared here. There was some there. But it was all over the floor. We had to put on-- we put shoe covers on in order to walk in the room. That was just how much it was.
<v Scott Shepherd>我看到到处都是血，满地都是。我当时真的惊呆了。血就这么糊在那儿。那儿也有一些。但到处都是。我们不得不穿上鞋套才能走进那个房间。血量大到这种程度。

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:45.650
<v Ira Glass>They walked in and saw a 26-year-old college student on the floor, handcuffed, with a bullet wound in his chest. He'd been tasered by police before he was shot.
<v Ira Glass>他们走进去，看到一名26岁的大学生躺在地上，戴着手铐，胸部有枪伤。他在被枪击前曾被警察用泰瑟枪电击过。

00:00:45.650 --> 00:01:20.510
<v Scott Shepherd>And he still had the taser things attached to his chest. And I mean, that was the first time I'd ever seen what taser lines look like-- just a bunch of wires coming from him. And I remember the surgeon screaming at the cop to take his handcuffs off. He was like, "take the cuffs off now. We have no time to waste." And he said it multiple times. "Really? He's cuffed on the floor? We can't do anything with him cuffed. Just take the damn cuffs off him."
<v Scott Shepherd>他的胸口还挂着泰瑟枪的电极。我是说，那是我第一次看到泰瑟枪的电线是什么样子——就是一堆连在他身上的线。我还记得外科医生对着警察大喊，让他把手铐解开。他说：“现在就把手铐解开。我们没时间浪费了。”他说了好几遍。“真的吗？他被拷在地上？他戴着手铐我们什么都做不了。快把那该死的手铐给他解开。”

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:46.980
<v Ira Glass>Police had shot an unarmed patient in his own hospital room. The fallout from this incident was enormous. Federal investigators came in. The hospital's funding was threatened. It was so contentious that I recorded an interview with this hospital staffer, and to protect their identity, we have replaced the person's voice with an actor saying the exact same words. The story I'm about to tell you is one that we put together in collaboration with The New York Times, last year. The actor, by the way, is named Scott Shepherd.
<v Ira Glass>警察在病房里枪击了一名手无寸铁的病人。这件事引发了巨大的影响。联邦调查人员介入了。医院的资金受到了威胁。这件事争议很大，我录制了对这名医院员工的采访，为了保护他们的身份，我们用演员的声音替换了当事人的声音，但内容一字不差。我接下来要讲的故事，是我们去年与《纽约时报》合作完成的。顺便提一下，这位演员叫斯科特·谢泼德。

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:58.430
<v Ira Glass>To explain what happened in that hospital room, I want to back up to the day before, 20 hours before the shooting, and tell you about the patient. The doctor saved his life. His name is Alan Pean. He's alive and talking about what happened.
<v Ira Glass>为了解释病房里发生的事，我想回溯到前一天，也就是枪击案发生前20小时，向你们介绍一下这位病人。医生挽救了他的生命。他的名字叫艾伦·皮恩。他还活着，并讲述了当时发生的事。

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:25.650
<v Ira Glass>And when he does, before you get to the very interesting story about how he ended up with a bullet in his chest, it's also incredibly interesting to hear him describe the day that led up to the shooting, because during that day, over the course of just a few hours, basically his mind slipped away from him. Bit by bit, he stopped understanding what was real and what wasn't real. And he describes what that feels like in this way I have never heard anybody describe before.
<v Ira Glass>当他讲述时，在你听到关于他胸部如何中枪的有趣故事之前，听他描述枪击案发生前的那一天也同样引人入胜，因为在那一天，在短短几个小时内，他的神志基本上离他而去。一点一点地，他不再能分清什么是真实的，什么是虚幻的。他用一种我从未听过的方式描述了那种感觉。

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:45.110
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life from WBEZ Chicago. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, "My Damn Mind." We have two stories on our program today-- in one, the brain working for you, and the other, this story, the brain very much not working for you, not being a helper, not being a pal. And let's get right to it.
<v Ira Glass>这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》节目。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目主题是“我该死的大脑”。我们今天有两个故事——一个讲述大脑为你工作，另一个，也就是这个故事，讲述大脑非常不为你工作，不是助手，也不是朋友。我们直接开始吧。

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:59.410
<v Ira Glass>This is Act One, "When Your Hospital-Borne Infection is a Bullet." The week before this happened, Alan Pean was starting his last semester at college, in a new school in a new city. He'd just moved to Houston two weeks before, anxious to do well.
<v Ira Glass>这是第一幕，“当你的院内感染是一颗子弹”。这件事发生前的一周，艾伦·皮恩刚开始大学的最后一个学期，在新的城市和新的学校。他两周前才搬到休斯顿，急于取得好成绩。

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:16.630
<v Ira Glass>Alan comes from a family with a lot of doctors-- his dad, his uncle. Both his brothers are on their way to becoming doctors. In this family of overachievers, Alan's the one who's still figuring out what he wants to do. He plays video games with a group of friends from high school, smokes weed, soft-spoken, thoughtful, considerate.
<v Ira Glass>艾伦出生于一个医生世家——他的父亲、叔叔。他的两个兄弟也都在成为医生的路上。在这个充满优等生的家庭里，艾伦是那个还在摸索自己想做什么的人。他会和高中好友一起打电子游戏，抽大麻，言语轻柔，深思熟虑，体贴入微。

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:37.010
<v Ira Glass>And he moved to Houston with a plan to get things done-- finish school, get to grad school. He stopped smoking weed, time to buckle down. And he was stressed out, sleeping only four hours a night. But at the same time, he had all this energy, which I guess is something that just happens to him sometimes.
<v Ira Glass>他带着目标搬到休斯顿——完成学业，读研究生。他戒掉了大麻，是时候努力了。他感到压力很大，每晚只睡四个小时。但与此同时，他浑身充满精力，我想这有时就是会发生在他身上的事。

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:46.260
<v Alan Pean>I recognize it as a mania. OK, Alan. You're manic right now. But I didn't think much of it. I was like, OK, this is just going to happen for a little bit, and it'll go away.
<v Alan Pean>我将其识别为躁狂发作。好吧，艾伦。你现在处于躁狂状态。但我没多想。我心想，好吧，这只会持续一会儿，它会过去的。

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:50.190
<v Ira Glass>And that's because in the past, sometimes this would happen for a couple of days, and then it would go away.
<v Ira Glass>那是因为在过去，这种情况有时会持续几天，然后就会消失。

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:51.940
<v Alan Pean>And it would go away. Exactly.
<v Alan Pean>而且它确实会消失。没错。

00:03:51.940 --> 00:04:00.120
<v Ira Glass>So he's trying to hold it together. He's going to class. He's forcing himself to talk to people at this new school. He's making himself do all the things that he's supposed to do.
<v Ira Glass>所以他试图保持状态。他去上课。他强迫自己与这所新学校的人交谈。他强迫自己去做所有他应该做的事。

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:11.030
<v Ira Glass>And it's working. On August 26, the day before the shooting, in his philosophy of science class, lots of people want to be in his study group. And when class ended, they made a plan to meet over the weekend.
<v Ira Glass>而且奏效了。8月26日，枪击案发生的前一天，在他的科学哲学课上，很多人想加入他的学习小组。课结束后，他们计划周末见面。

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:32.670
<v Alan Pean>And everyone was exchanging emails. And I was like, "all right, OK. So I'll see you guys later." And you know, it's like, we were in the quad, and I'm walking out to go to my car. And I just continue walking. I feel great. This is good. I finally have my group ready to go. I'm going to go ahead and get home and continue being productive. I have a book that I really like to read. I'm going to go finish that. And then--
<v Alan Pean>大家都在交换电子邮件。我就说：“好吧，那我们待会儿见。”你知道，当时我们在方庭，我正走去开车。我继续走着。我感觉很棒。这很好。我终于组建好我的团队了。我要回家继续保持高产。我有一本很喜欢的书。我要把它读完。然后——

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:45.900
<v Ira Glass>This is the moment that we're roughly 20 hours before the shooting in the hospital room. When Alan gets in the car, he starts thinking about all the things that he needs to read and do and study, and something in him just kind of tips, he says.
<v Ira Glass>这就是距离病房枪击案大约20小时的时刻。当艾伦上车时，他开始想到所有需要阅读、处理和研究的事情，他说，他内心的某种东西崩溃了。

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:58.840
<v Ira Glass>He starts to panic. But you know, he gets home, he starts to cook some food, he tries to calm down. He's got a plan to play a video game online with his buddies. He hooks up the PS4, gets on FaceTime with his friends.
<v Ira Glass>他开始恐慌。但他回到家，开始做些食物，试图冷静下来。他计划和好友在线玩游戏。他接上PS4，和朋友进行FaceTime视频通话。

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:13.940
<v Alan Pean>So I'm trying to play on the game, but it's like my wires start getting crossed. Things start getting confusing. Confusing like I have this controller, but then a thought would interject.
<v Alan Pean>我试图玩游戏，但就像我的线路短路了一样。事情开始变得混乱。混乱到我手里拿着手柄，但有一个念头会插进来。

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:20.260
<v Ira Glass>He thought this controller somehow switches on a processor inside of him.
<v Ira Glass>他认为这个手柄以某种方式启动了他体内的处理器。

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:38.680
<v Alan Pean>Like I'm this bionic being who had been reprogrammed by the enemy. It's like where you have the delusional thoughts competing with your rational thoughts. And then you disproving your delusional thoughts, but then another one comes up. And it seems more likely.
<v Alan Pean>就像我是一个被敌人重新编程的仿生人。就像妄想思维与理性思维在竞争。你反驳了妄想思维，但另一个又出现了。而且它看起来更真实。

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:50.680
<v Ira Glass>His computer, he thought, was not his computer. It was a state-of-the-art device that could summon drones to destroy his apartment complex. Meaningless coincidences seemed like signals that he was on a secret mission.
<v Ira Glass>他认为他的电脑不是电脑。而是一台尖端设备，可以召唤无人机摧毁他的公寓大楼。毫无意义的巧合似乎成了他在执行秘密任务的信号。

00:05:50.680 --> 00:06:00.800
<v Ira Glass>Now, Alan had had delusional episodes before-- two times. The first was in 2008, when he was a freshman at the University of Texas at Austin. It lasted about a week.
<v Ira Glass>艾伦之前有过妄想症发作——两次。第一次是在2008年，他在德克萨斯大学奥斯汀分校读大一的时候。持续了大约一周。

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:14.170
<v Alan Pean>Where I thought that I was President Barack Obama. That I was either a lookalike or someone that could be used as a replacement. But it's not so much like--
<v Alan Pean>当时我以为我是巴拉克·奥巴马总统。要么我长得像他，要么我是可以被当作替身的人。但这并不是说——

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:20.750
<v Ira Glass>I should tell you that you're a light-skinned black man with short hair. You look nothing like President Barack Obama.
<v Ira Glass>我得告诉你，你是一个皮肤白皙、头发短的黑人男性。你看起来一点也不像巴拉克·奥巴马总统。

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:21.870
<v Alan Pean>Yeah. So--
<v Alan Pean>是的。所以——

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:37.630
<v Ira Glass>The next summer, it happened again. And it was more of a biblical-type deal this time, Alan says, a delusion with demons in disguise, and he was on the verge of being sent to hell. Alan was briefly hospitalized, diagnosed as possible bipolar and put on anti-psychotic medications for a while.
<v Ira Glass>第二年夏天，它又发生了。艾伦说，这次更像是一个圣经类型的事情，一种伪装成恶魔的妄想，他处于被打入地狱的边缘。艾伦曾短暂住院，被诊断为双相情感障碍，并服用了一段时间的抗精神病药物。

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:48.950
<v Ira Glass>And he recovered. Six years passed without another incident. But that night, on the PS4 with his friends on FaceTime, it was all kicking in again.
<v Ira Glass>然后他康复了。六年过去了，没有再发生事故。但那天晚上，在PS4上和朋友FaceTime时，一切又开始了。

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:53.330
<v Ira Glass>And what are your friends seeing? Do they know you're going through this freak-out?
<v Ira Glass>你朋友们看到了什么？他们知道你正在经历这种崩溃吗？

00:06:53.330 --> 00:07:05.950
<v Alan Pean>They start asking, Alan, what's wrong? They had realized that I was acting strange. Like, why can't he log on to the computer? Why can't he log on to the PS4 or whatever? Why can't he do that?
<v Alan Pean>他们开始问，艾伦，你怎么了？他们意识到我的行为很奇怪。比如，为什么他不能登录电脑？为什么他不能登录PS4什么的？为什么他做不到？

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:07.380
<v Ira Glass>Oh, because you're unable to do it?
<v Ira Glass>哦，因为你做不到？

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:14.530
<v Alan Pean>Yeah. I was like, no. I shouldn't do it, because if I do, they'll triangulate on my position.
<v Alan Pean>是的。我当时心想，不。我不应该这么做，因为如果我做了，他们就会确定我的位置。

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:15.180
<v Ira Glass>But you didn't say that to your friends?
<v Ira Glass>但你没跟朋友说这些？

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:19.030
<v Alan Pean>I couldn't tell it to them because I didn't know who exactly they were.
<v Alan Pean>我没法告诉他们，因为我不知道他们到底是谁。

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:23.620
<v Ira Glass>So he gets off the FaceTime call. It's now 13 hours before the shooting.
<v Ira Glass>于是他挂断了FaceTime电话。现在距离枪击案还有13小时。

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:42.300
<v Alan Pean>And I was like, who can I trust? Well, obviously, you trust your brother, your unit, right? So he's my little brother, Dominique. And I call him and I tell him, "hey, man, I'm having a panic attack. I'm having a panic attack. And I don't know what to do. And I think it's getting worse than that."
<v Alan Pean>我心想，我能信任谁？好吧，显然，你信任你的兄弟，你的血亲，对吧？所以他是我的弟弟，多米尼克。我给他打电话，我说：“嘿，兄弟，我正在经历恐慌发作。我正在恐慌发作。我不知道该怎么办。而且我觉得情况比那更糟。”

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:47.110
<v Dominique Pean>So he called me at 10:00 PM. I had just got back from class.
<v Dominique Pean>所以他在晚上10点给我打了电话。我刚从课上回来。

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:58.070
<v Ira Glass>Dominique says, actually, there's a fair amount of "how was your day" kind of small talk with Alan taking these weird, deep, deep breaths before Alan actually admitted he was having a panic attack.
<v Ira Glass>多米尼克说，实际上，在艾伦承认他正在恐慌发作之前，他们聊了一些“你今天过得怎么样”之类的闲话，期间艾伦会有那种奇怪的、深深的呼吸。

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:13.180
<v Dominique Pean>And I was like, "oh, OK." Well, I get anxious kind of often, too. So I walked him through some just relaxation exercises. So I was like, take a deep breath-- in, out. Lie on your back. Put some water on your face.
<v Dominique Pean>我说：“哦，好吧。”嗯，我也经常感到焦虑。所以我引导他做了一些放松练习。我对他说，深呼吸——吸气，呼气。平躺。往脸上泼点水。

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:14.710
<v Ira Glass>Water on your face? Like cold water?
<v Ira Glass>往脸上泼水？像冷水那样？

00:08:14.710 --> 00:08:16.000
<v Dominique Pean>Yeah. Cold water on your face.
<v Dominique Pean>是的。往脸上泼冷水。

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:28.640
<v Alan Pean>And he was like, "oh, OK. Well, put some cold water on your face. Cold water on your face." And I was like, "what?" And I'm like-- I actually go to say-- I'm, like, "is it because I'm overheating?"
<v Alan Pean>他说：“哦，好的。好吧，往脸上泼冷水。往脸上泼冷水。”我心想，“什么？”我实际上想说——我心想，“是因为我过热了吗？”

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:30.360
<v Ira Glass>Like you're some sort of robot?
<v Ira Glass>就像你是某种机器人？

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:42.680
<v Alan Pean>Right. And he might also know that. And he knows what I need to do in order for my circuits to calm down. [SIGH]
<v Alan Pean>对。而且他可能也知道这一点。他知道为了让我的电路冷静下来，我需要做什么。[叹气]

00:08:42.680 --> 00:09:02.880
<v Ira Glass>It continues like this. The part of Alan's brain that looks at the world and draws conclusions about what he sees doesn't stop doing that. It's like he's still in there, thinking and applying logic. It just keeps arriving at the wrong answers. So innocent statements said to him, like "I don't know anybody in Houston who can come over to your apartment," Alan inserts into the delusion.
<v Ira Glass>事情就是这样持续下去。艾伦大脑中观察世界并对其所得结论进行判断的部分并没有停止工作。就像他依然在里面，思考并应用逻辑。只是它总是在得出错误的答案。所以对他说出的无辜陈述，比如“我不认识休斯顿有人能到你的公寓来”，艾伦都会将其植入妄想中。

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:20.570
<v Alan Pean>I took that as code. There's no agents in your area that could come to your assistance. Then I'm thinking, like, well, wait a minute. Like, what if-- what if he's not really my brother? It could be some sort of trap.
<v Alan Pean>我把它当作暗号。你们区域没有特工能来支援。然后我心想，等等。万一——万一他不是我兄弟呢？这可能是某种陷阱。

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:25.450
<v Ira Glass>Alan hangs up on his brother. Dominique gets their dad to reach out. Here he is.
<v Ira Glass>艾伦挂断了他兄弟的电话。多米尼克找他们的父亲介入。这就是他。

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:30.740
<v Dr. Harold Pean>So I call him, and I try to calm him through it on the phone to try to relax him.
<v Dr. Harold Pean>所以我给他打电话，试图在电话里安抚他，让他放松下来。

00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:35.880
<v Ira Glass>This is Alan's dad, Dr. Harold Pean. He's originally from Haiti, hence the accent.
<v Ira Glass>这是艾伦的父亲，哈罗德·皮恩医生。他原籍海地，所以有口音。

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:55.620
<v Dr. Harold Pean>I ask him, "Alan, how you doing? You're having some problem, they're telling me," and try to relax him to get his mind off. But he was very anxious. He was not able to focus, to control it. And then suddenly, he say, "well, I think I'm going to just go drive to the hospital or something."
<v Dr. Harold Pean>我问他：“艾伦，你好吗？他们告诉我你遇到了一些问题，”试图让他放松，转移注意力。但他非常焦虑。他无法集中精神，无法控制。然后他突然说：“好吧，我想我还是开车去医院什么的吧。”

00:09:55.620 --> 00:10:11.400
<v Ira Glass>Alan doesn't tell his dad or his brother that he's having delusions. He doesn't say what he's really thinking, which is that he's in greater and greater danger if he stays at his apartment, that there are snipers outside watching the front of the building, that if he walks out his front door he'll get shot, and they're moving in.
<v Ira Glass>艾伦没有告诉父亲或兄弟他正在遭受妄想。他没有说他内心真实的想法，即如果他留在公寓里，他会处于越来越大的危险中；窗外有狙击手监视着楼前；如果他走出前门，他会被枪杀，他们正在逼近。

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:40.400
<v Alan Pean>I thought at that point that time elapsed too long and that they're closing in on my position, and they're going to be there any moment. So I'm talking with my dad, and I call him Pop. So I'm like, Pop-- and then the following word in my mind was pop, which made me believe that the phone was going to explode, right? And then I run out, throw my phone into the toilet, and run out into the balcony and close the balcony door behind me.
<v Alan Pean>当时我觉得时间拖得太久了，他们正在包围我的位置，随时都会到。所以我正和我父亲谈话，我叫他“老爸”。所以我心想，老爸——然后我脑海中接下来的词是“砰”（pop），这让我相信电话会爆炸，对吧？然后我跑出去，把手机扔进马桶，跑到阳台上，关上阳台门。

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:59.780
<v Alan Pean>So I'm on the balcony. It's the third-story balcony. And I'm like, OK, well, just remember your training. And I start to climb over on the other side and essentially let go and catch myself again on the second balcony area. And--
<v Alan Pean>所以我在阳台上。这是三楼的阳台。我心想，好吧，记住你的训练。我开始爬到另一边，基本上松手，然后又抓住了二楼的阳台区域。然后——

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:00.380
