<v Alan Pean>报废了。

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:46.120
<v Ira Glass>You just rammed into--
<v Ira Glass>你只是撞到了——

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:48.770
<v Alan Pean>The side of a wall.
<v Alan Pean>一面墙的侧面。

00:14:48.770 --> 00:15:00.030
<v Ira Glass>He had actually hit a bunch of cars before he slammed into the side of the hospital at high speed. The front of the car caved in, airbags inflated. Even in his psychotic state, Alan wore a seat belt.
<v Ira Glass>他在高速撞向医院侧面之前，实际上已经撞了好几辆车。车头塌陷，气囊弹出。即使在精神错乱的状态下，艾伦还是系了安全带。

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:11.020
<v Alan Pean>I mean, I'm religious. I thank God that I didn't hurt anyone else there. I thank God that people there just miraculously did not get hit at all.
<v Alan Pean>我是说，我有宗教信仰。我感谢上帝我没有伤害到那里的人。我感谢上帝，那里的人奇迹般地完全没有被撞到。

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:16.360
<v Ira Glass>So you get out of the car. You walk to the hospital, which is how far from you?
<v Ira Glass>所以你下了车。你走到医院，离你有多远？

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:22.790
<v Alan Pean>10 feet. I'm at the hospital. I crashed into the hospital. And there's an EMT guy right there.
<v Alan Pean>10英尺。我在医院。我撞进了医院。那里正好有个急救人员。

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:29.810
<v Ira Glass>The EMT sees that Alan just crashed his car and probably needs medical attention. They put Alan on a stretcher, take him into the ER.
<v Ira Glass>急救人员看到艾伦刚撞了车，可能需要医疗救治。他们把艾伦放在担架上，送进了急诊室。

00:15:29.810 --> 00:15:56.600
<v Ira Glass>This is where this turns from one kind of story, a story of Alan losing control of his mind, to the other story we're telling here, of how you can show up in an emergency room needing psychiatric help and end up shot by the police in the hospital. And from this point in the story, for the 11 hours before the shooting, we don't have to rely on Alan's recollection. There's documentation of what he looked like from the outside to people who were not in his head, people who did not think that he was a robot cyborg on a secret mission.
<v Ira Glass>这正是故事从一种类型——艾伦失去理智的故事，转变为我们在这里讲述的另一种故事的地方：你如何能因为需要精神病学帮助而出现在急诊室，最终却被医院里的警察枪击。从故事的这一点，即枪击案发生前11小时，我们不必依赖艾伦的回忆。有书面记录显示，从那些不在他脑子里、不认为他是执行秘密任务的机器人半机械人的眼中，他看起来是什么样子的。

00:15:56.600 --> 00:16:07.470
<v Ira Glass>We have Alan's medical records. He gave permission for the hospital to turn them over. And there's a 50-page report, prepared by federal investigators, of what happened from the time Alan arrived at the hospital until he was shot.
<v Ira Glass>我们有艾伦的医疗记录。他授权医院将记录移交。联邦调查人员准备了一份50页的报告，记录了从艾伦到达医院到他被枪击期间发生的事情。

00:16:07.470 --> 00:16:20.370
<v Ira Glass>Alan gets to the emergency room at 10 minutes before midnight. And St. Joseph is a big downtown hospital. It gets lots of trauma patients, lots of mentally ill patients, some homeless people. Staff often have to quickly assess what a patient needs to be treated for.
<v Ira Glass>艾伦在午夜前10分钟到达急诊室。圣约瑟夫是一家大型市中心医院。它接收很多创伤病人、很多精神病患者，还有一些无家可归的人。员工通常必须迅速评估病人需要治疗什么。

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:36.900
<v Ira Glass>And even though the staff notices all kinds of things that might lead them to treat Alan for his psychiatric problem, they don't do it. What they treat Alan for is the car crash, which must have seemed pretty urgent. The diagnosis in his medical chart is hand abrasion, substance abuse, motor vehicle accident.
<v Ira Glass>尽管工作人员注意到了各种可能导致他们治疗艾伦精神问题的迹象，但他们并没有这样做。他们治疗艾伦的是车祸，这看起来肯定非常紧急。他病历上的诊断是手部擦伤、物质滥用、机动车事故。

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:53.270
<v Ira Glass>They do a CT scan of his head, spine, chest, abdomen, and pelvis, looking for injuries. They test him for drugs and alcohol. Those come up negative, except for marijuana, which can linger in the body for weeks. Alan says that he tried to tell the ER staff what it is that he came for, that he was having a manic episode.
<v Ira Glass>他们对他进行了头部、脊柱、胸部、腹部和骨盆的CT扫描，寻找损伤。他们对他进行了药物和酒精测试。测试结果呈阴性，除了大麻，大麻可以在体内残留数周。艾伦说他试图告诉急诊室工作人员他为什么而来，他正在经历躁狂发作。

00:16:53.270 --> 00:17:07.950
<v Alan Pean>It's like I snapped back into myself. I just say, "I'm manic, I'm manic, I'm manic." And I say it multiple times. And I'm saying that. And there's people around me. There's nurses and stuff who are putting needles and things into me. And it never clicked.
<v Alan Pean>就像我恢复了清醒。我只是说，“我躁狂，我躁狂，我躁狂。”我说了好几次。我当时就在说。我周围有人。有护士之类的，在给我打针。但他们从未听进去。

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:23.940
<v Ira Glass>There's no mention of that in his medical records. And there's no mention of phone calls that his brother Christian and his father say they made at 1:00 AM and 1:14 AM, respectively, to alert the hospital about Alan's psychiatric issues-- though that's not unusual that phone calls like that wouldn't be mentioned in the chart.
<v Ira Glass>他的医疗记录中没有提到这一点。而且没有提到他的兄弟克里斯蒂安和父亲分别在凌晨1点和1点14分打的那些电话，试图提醒医院艾伦的精神问题——尽管这种电话没有在病历中提到并不罕见。

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:56.520
<v Ira Glass>But the medical records do include all kinds of things that could have tipped the staff off about what was really wrong. On Alan's arrival at the ER, the first handwritten notes from a resident on duty include that Alan is someone who has manic depression. Then Alan is so incoherent, making so little sense, that the attending physician notes in the chart that he can't get Alan to describe his medical history or even a basic rundown of his symptoms. By 1:30 in the morning, the nurses' notes say that Alan is acting weirdly, trying to pull out his IVs and his lines. They, quote, "educate him on his behavior and ask him not to remove any equipment."
<v Ira Glass>但医疗记录确实包括了各种可能提醒工作人员他真正出了什么问题的迹象。在艾伦到达急诊室时，当班住院医师的第一份手写记录包括艾伦是一个有躁狂抑郁症的人。然后艾伦语无伦次，逻辑不通，以至于主治医师在图表中记录，他无法让艾伦描述他的病史，甚至无法进行基本的症状总结。到了凌晨1点半，护士的记录显示艾伦表现得很奇怪，试图拔掉他的静脉注射管和导线。她们“教育了他关于他的行为，并要求他不要移除任何设备”。

00:17:56.520 --> 00:18:06.410
<v Ira Glass>He doesn't stop. He tries to pull out his own catheter. This continues for hours. He starts telling the staff that he feels all better, no need for any equipment.
<v Ira Glass>他没有停止。他试图拔掉自己的导尿管。这种情况持续了数小时。他开始告诉工作人员他感觉好多了，不需要任何设备。

00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:25.280
<v Ira Glass>But they still don't treat him like a psychiatric case. Alan and his family told me, their sense of the staff's attitude was, this is just a guy, drunk or on drugs, crashed his car. Happens all the time. Alan's put into a hospital room for observation of his injuries. His father booked a plane to Houston from their home in McAllen, Texas.
<v Ira Glass>但他们仍然没有把他当作精神病病例对待。艾伦和他的家人告诉我，他们对工作人员态度的感觉是：这只是个喝醉或吸毒的人，撞了车。这种情况常有。艾伦被送入观察病房观察伤势。他父亲预订了一张从他们家所在的德克萨斯州麦卡伦飞往休斯顿的机票。

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:31.560
<v Dr. Harold Pean>We decide to fly right away, yeah, 6:45. We went to Houston at 6:45 in the morning.
<v Dr. Harold Pean>我们决定马上飞过去，是的，6:45。我们早上6:45去了休斯顿。

