<v Ira Glass>Dr. Pean and his wife, Paloma, get to Alan's hospital room around 10:00 AM.
<v Ira Glass>皮恩医生和他的妻子帕洛玛在早上10点左右到达艾伦的病房。

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:49.440
<v Dr. Harold Pean>Well, I say, "Alan, we are here. You know, we've come to see you." He say, "hi, Pop." But he would not-- I say, "how you doing?" He would not tell me anything. And he was different than usual. So I say, well, that's not him.
<v Dr. Harold Pean>嗯，我说，“艾伦，我们到了。你知道，我们来看你了。”他说，“嗨，老爸。”但他不会——我说，“你感觉怎么样？”他什么也不肯告诉我不。他和平常不一样。所以我说，好吧，那不是他。

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:54.260
<v Ira Glass>He's totally withdrawn. Didn't hug them hello. Alan explains why.
<v Ira Glass>他完全退缩了。没有拥抱他们表示问候。艾伦解释了原因。

00:18:54.260 --> 00:19:02.550
<v Alan Pean>I do recall my parents showing up. And I don't actually believe it's them. I don't think it's them.
<v Alan Pean>我确实记得我父母出现了。但我实际上不相信那是他们。我不认为那是他们。

00:19:02.550 --> 00:19:11.740
<v Ira Glass>In Alan's mind, he's still on his mission, which is evolving. The TV in his room is sending coded messages. And the IV tubes he's hooked up to?
<v Ira Glass>在艾伦的脑海里，他仍在执行他的任务，而任务在演变。病房里的电视在发送编码信息。他接上的静脉导管？

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:26.480
<v Alan Pean>They're putting the serum that I needed in order for the morphing process to begin, to morph, to look more like Barack Obama. Because if he is taken out, they don't know where he is. But they need someone to speak out as though the government is still functioning as normal.
<v Alan Pean>它们正在输入我开始变形过程所需的血清，去变形，看起来更像巴拉克·奥巴马。因为如果他被除掉了，他们不知道他在哪。但他们需要有人以政府依然正常运转的姿态发言。

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:33.170
<v Ira Glass>Now his dad knows none of that. All he knows is something is wrong. He goes to the nurses' station.
<v Ira Glass>他父亲对这一切一无所知。他只知道出了问题。他去了护士站。

00:19:33.170 --> 00:19:47.610
<v Dr. Harold Pean>So I talk with the nurse, and I told them, look, I think he's having some kind of anxiety or panic, and I wanted to talk with the doctor. And I say to her that I think he may need some kind of psychiatric help.
<v Dr. Harold Pean>所以我跟护士谈话，我告诉他们，看，我觉得他有某种焦虑或恐慌，我想跟医生谈谈。我告诉她我觉得他可能需要某种精神病学帮助。

00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:50.330
<v Ira Glass>Did you say, I want a psychiatric evaluation?
<v Ira Glass>你说了“我想要精神病评估”吗？

00:19:50.330 --> 00:19:55.050
<v Dr. Harold Pean>That's right. I told her, I think he needs to be evaluated by a psychiatrist.
<v Dr. Harold Pean>没错。我告诉她，我觉得他需要接受精神科医生的评估。

00:19:55.050 --> 00:19:57.210
<v Ira Glass>And what did the nurse say?
<v Ira Glass>护士说了什么？

00:19:57.210 --> 00:20:05.050
<v Dr. Harold Pean>Oh, she just didn't pay attention to me. She said he's going to be discharged. That's all she said.
<v Dr. Harold Pean>哦，她根本没理我。她说他马上就要出院了。这就是她说的全部。

00:20:05.050 --> 00:20:12.550
<v Ira Glass>According to the report by federal investigators, the nurse confirmed he did ask for a psychiatric evaluation, and she did tell him Alan was going to be discharged.
<v Ira Glass>根据联邦调查人员的报告，护士证实他确实要求进行精神病评估，并且她确实告诉他艾伦要出院了。

00:20:12.550 --> 00:20:22.880
<v Dr. Harold Pean>And I asked what medication they were giving him. They said they were giving him cyclobenzaprine. And I told her, well, I don't think that's appropriate. Maybe that will make it worse.
<v Dr. Harold Pean>我问他们给他开了什么药。他们说给他用了环苯扎林。我告诉她，嗯，我觉得那不合适。也许那会使情况更糟。

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:25.600
<v Ira Glass>What was your fear that it would do to Alan?
<v Ira Glass>你担心那会对艾伦做什么？

00:20:25.600 --> 00:20:45.500
<v Dr. Harold Pean>That's a muscle relaxant. But some people, it may cause psychosis, actually, because that's part of the side-effect-- confusion, dizziness, psychosis. I think it would probably make him more disoriented, confused. But she said, well, that's what the doctor prescribed.
<v Dr. Harold Pean>那是一种肌肉松弛剂。但对有些人来说，它可能会导致精神错乱，因为这是副作用的一部分——精神混乱、头晕、精神错乱。我觉得它可能会让他更加迷失方向、困惑。但她说，好吧，那是医生开的药。

00:20:45.500 --> 00:20:47.740
<v Ira Glass>Did you identify yourself as a doctor?
<v Ira Glass>你表明你是医生了吗？

00:20:47.740 --> 00:21:00.500
<v Dr. Harold Pean>Yeah. I told the nurse that I'm a physician from McAllen, internal medicine. But she just didn't pay attention. She thought maybe I was lying. I don't know what was in her head.
<v Dr. Harold Pean>是的。我告诉护士我是来自麦卡伦的内科医生。但她根本没在意。她以为我可能在撒谎。我不知道她在想什么。

00:21:00.500 --> 00:21:15.980
<v Ira Glass>He said the nurse said to him-- maybe hearing his accent-- you might be a doctor somewhere, but you're not a doctor here. So Dr. Pean and his wife figured, OK, no help here. They would drive Alan back home to McAllen and get psychiatric help there. They left to arrange for a car rental.
<v Ira Glass>他说护士对他说——也许听到他的口音——你可能是哪里的医生，但你不是这儿的医生。所以皮恩医生和他的妻子觉得，好吧，这里没帮助。他们会开车把艾伦带回麦卡伦，在那里寻求精神病学帮助。他们离开去安排租车事宜。

00:21:15.980 --> 00:21:29.620
<v Ira Glass>The doctor did come looking for them, according to federal investigators, but they'd already left. Alan gets shot in his own hospital room less than 40 minutes after they go. Here's how that goes down. I'm going to read you a little bit from the 50-page report issued by federal investigators.
<v Ira Glass>根据联邦调查人员的说法，医生确实来找过他们，但他们已经离开了。他们在离开不到40分钟后，艾伦就在自己的病房里被枪击了。事情经过是这样的。我将为你朗读联邦调查人员发布的50页报告中的一小部分。

00:21:29.620 --> 00:21:53.470
<v Ira Glass>Quote, "At 11 o'clock, the patient was encouraged to put on his gown after he had a shower, but refused and started dancing in his room. The patient came out of his room naked four to five times. The nurse stated at this time, the patient was calmly responsive. She stated each time she redirected him, he would say, yes, ma'am. Radio. OK, ma'am. When asked what year it was, he said, 1989.
<v Ira Glass>引用，“11点，病人在洗完澡后被鼓励穿上病号服，但他拒绝了，并开始在房间里跳舞。病人裸体走出房间四到五次。护士表示此时病人反应冷静。她表示每次她引导他回来，他都会说，是的，女士。明白。好的，女士。当被问到今年是哪一年，他说，1989年。”

00:21:53.470 --> 00:22:06.480
<v Ira Glass>Some time later, the patient came out in the hallway naked, except he had shoes on. He was told to go back to his room, and the nurse would come in to assist him with his gown. She stated she went to the room with another nurse and was trying to help the patient into his gown.
<v Ira Glass>过了一段时间，病人裸体出现在走廊上，除了脚上穿着鞋。他被告知回到房间，护士会进去协助他穿上病号服。她表示她和另一名护士去了房间，正试图帮助病人穿上病号服。

00:22:06.480 --> 00:22:22.000
<v Ira Glass>They told him to turn around so they could tie the gown. The patient started to dance and would not comply. The nurse said the patient was acting inappropriately. She said he would not comply with her orders to turn around so his gown could be buttoned up."
<v Ira Glass>她们告诉他转身以便系上病号服。病人开始跳舞，不肯配合。护士说病人表现不恰当。她说他不配合她转身穿上病号服的命令。”

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:25.040
<v Ira Glass>Do you remember why you were going out into the hallway naked?
<v Ira Glass>你还记得为什么你要裸体走到走廊上吗？

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:34.530
<v Alan Pean>I don't remember-- I don't recall dancing, but I do know that I was going out there because whenever I finished taking my showers, well, where's the suit?
<v Alan Pean>我不记得——我不记得跳舞，但我确实知道我走出去是因为每当我洗完澡，好吧，西装在哪儿？

00:22:34.530 --> 00:22:42.910
<v Ira Glass>Remember, he's morphing into Barack Obama. He's about to address the nation. You need a suit for that kind of thing. In the hallway--
<v Ira Glass>记住，他正在变身为巴拉克·奥巴马。他即将向全国发表讲话。那种场合需要一套西装。在走廊里——

00:22:42.910 --> 00:22:54.740
<v Alan Pean>I'd expect other people to be there that would help me with the changing process, just to look professional or to hand me my script.
<v Alan Pean>我会期待其他人也在那里帮助我完成变形过程，只是为了看起来职业一点，或者把讲稿递给我。

00:22:54.740 --> 00:23:13.230
<v Ira Glass>If the patient doesn't comply with orders, it's protocol in the hospital to ring for security. And the nurse does that. Now at this particular hospital, calling security usually gets you uniformed guards who don't carry weapons, but also might get you off-duty cops who are on the security team. It depends on who's available.
<v Ira Glass>如果病人不配合命令，医院的规程是呼叫安保。护士照做了。而在这一家特定的医院，呼叫安保通常来的是不带武器的穿制服警卫，但也可能来的是安保团队里的休班警察。取决于谁有空。

00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:29.690
<v Ira Glass>This time, it's the cops who arrive, in full Houston Police Department uniforms with guns. They go into his room and close the door. This, by the way, is not standard hospital procedure, I was told. A nurse, a doctor, somebody treating the patient, should  have been in there with them.
<v Ira Glass>这一次，来的是警察，身穿休斯顿警察局的制服，配有枪支。他们走进他的房间并关上了门。顺便说一下，有人告诉我，这不是标准的医院程序。一名护士、医生，或者任何正在治疗该病人的工作人员，应该和他们一起进去。

00:23:29.690 --> 00:23:43.660
<v Ira Glass>Now at this point, once the cops are inside that room, the story gets a little hazy. It's not totally clear what happened inside the room. Police turned down our interview request. And Alan's lawyers told him not to talk about the details either because the whole thing is going to court.
<v Ira Glass>现在，一旦警察进了那个房间，故事就变得有点模糊了。房间里到底发生了什么并不完全清楚。警方拒绝了我们的采访请求。艾伦的律师也告诉他不要谈论细节，因为整件事都要上法庭。

00:23:43.660 --> 00:24:00.130
<v Ira Glass>But there are some things we know for sure. We know the cops were not in that room for long, a few minutes at most. We know there was a scuffle. Several people told investigators that. And the staffer I interviewed, whose voice we're replacing with an actor's, said you  could hear it from outside Alan's hospital room.
<v Ira Glass>但有些事情我们是确定的。我们知道警察在那个房间里待的时间不长，最多几分钟。我们知道发生了扭打。几个人告诉调查人员这件事。我采访的那名员工——我们用演员的声音替换了他的——说你在艾伦病房外就能听到声音。

00:24:00.130 --> 00:24:08.610
<v Scott Shepherd>We heard noise. And we couldn't identify exactly what it was. We didn't know if it was maybe a table hitting a wall. it was just like a pop and all kinds of stuff.
<v Scott Shepherd>我们听到了噪音。我们无法确定那到底是什么。我们不知道那是否可能是桌子撞到墙的声音。就像砰的一声，然后是各种东西。

00:24:08.610 --> 00:24:18.590
<v Ira Glass>One nurse told investigators she heard a scream. On the day of the shooting  in August, the Houston Police Department issued a brief statement about what happened next. It's still on their website.
<v Ira Glass>一名护士告诉调查人员她听到了尖叫声。在8月份枪击案发生当天，休斯顿警察局就接下来的情况发表了一份简短的声明。它仍然在他们的网站上。

00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:44.040
<v Ira Glass>It says, when the officers arrived, Alan attacked. Quote, "The patient suddenly physically assaulted Officer Ortega, striking him in the head, causing a laceration. At that time, Officer Law deployed his  conducted  energy device--" that's the taser-- "which had no apparent effect on the suspect who continued to assault the officers. Officer Ortega, fearing for his and his partner's safety, then discharged his duty weapon, striking the subject one time."
<v Ira Glass>声明称，当警察到达时，艾伦发动了攻击。引用，“病人突然对奥尔特加警官进行人身攻击，击中了他的头部，造成裂伤。当时，劳警官部署了他的警用导电装置——也就是泰瑟枪——这对嫌疑人没有产生明显的抑制作用，嫌疑人继续攻击警察。奥尔特加警官担心自己和搭档的安全，随后发射了配枪，击中嫌疑人一次。”

00:24:44.040 --> 00:24:57.520
<v Ira Glass>Alan disputes this account. He and his lawyers say he did not attack the officers first but they charged him first, and he responded.
<v Ira Glass>艾伦对这个说法有异议。他和他的律师说他没有先攻击警察，而是他们先冲向他，他作出了反应。

00:24:57.520 --> 00:25:15.480
<v Ira Glass>Soon the door to his hospital room opened. That, by the way, is when the hospital staffer who I interviewed arrived in the hallway outside Alan's room. Again, we've replaced the staffer's voice with an actor saying the same words and-- I hope this isn't weird-- you're hearing my original questions from the recording of the original interview mixed with the actor's voice.
<v Ira Glass>很快他病房的门开了。顺便说一下，那正是我采访的那名医院员工到达艾伦病房外走廊的时候。同样，我们用演员的声音替换了该员工的声音，说的是同样的话，而且——我希望这不奇怪——你听到的是录音中我原来的提问，混杂着演员的声音。

00:25:15.480 --> 00:25:23.460
<v Scott Shepherd>Apparently, they had probably just opened it. And the first thing I seen was a cop fall out of the room on the floor.
<v Scott Shepherd>显然，他们可能刚打开门。我看到的第一件事是一个警察从房间里摔倒在地。

00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:25.460
<v Ira Glass>So you're out in the hallway.
<v Ira Glass>所以你在走廊上。

00:25:25.460 --> 00:25:33.440
<v Scott Shepherd>Correct. Well, I didn't go that close to the room because when you see a cop with a bloody eye on the floor, that states, stay where you are. Don't go any closer.
<v Scott Shepherd>没错。嗯，我没有靠房间太近，因为当你看到一个警察眼睛带血躺在地上时，那就意味着，待在你原来的地方。不要再靠近。

00:25:33.440 --> 00:25:34.940
<v Ira Glass>He was bleeding from his eye?
<v Ira Glass>他在流血，眼睛吗？

00:25:34.940 --> 00:25:47.420
<v Scott Shepherd>His head. Like right above his right eye. We weren't sure if it was the eye or the head. It was just this huge, red, fresh gash. It was big.
<v Scott Shepherd>他的头。就在右眼上方。我们不确定是眼睛还是头。就只是那个巨大的、红色的、新鲜的伤口。它很大。

00:25:47.420 --> 00:26:02.890
<v Ira Glass>This is from the account by the federal investigators. Quote, "Inside the room, the other officer was standing over the patient, who was lying on the floor. He had a taser on his upper chest. There was blood on the wall and floor. The patient was conscious, trying to get up, saying he was Super-Man and needed to get up.
<v Ira Glass>这是来自联邦调查人员的报告。引用，“在房间里，另一名警察站在躺在地上的病人上方。他的上胸部有一把泰瑟枪。墙上和地板上有血。病人有意识，试图起身，说他是超人，需要起来。”

00:26:02.890 --> 00:26:19.850
<v Ira Glass>The doctor who rushed into the room and treated him told investigators--" again, I'm quoting the federal report here, quote-- "he  heard a Code Blue page overhead while he was walking upstairs. He realized it was his patient's room and went to the floor. He went to the patient's room and saw him lying on the floor on his back with a drape over him.
<v Ira Glass>冲进房间并治疗他的医生告诉调查人员——再次引用联邦报告，引用——“他在走上楼时听到了头顶的蓝色警报广播。他意识到是病人的房间，并赶到了那一层。他去了病人的房间，看到他仰面躺在地上，身上盖着布。”

00:26:19.850 --> 00:26:30.330
<v Ira Glass>The patient was quiet. His eyes were closed. The doctor says he took the drape off the patient and saw he was handcuffed. When he asked what happened, he was told the patient was just tasered.
<v Ira Glass>病人很安静。眼睛闭着。医生说他把病人身上的布拿开，看到他戴着手铐。当他问发生了什么时，得到的回答是病人刚被泰瑟枪电击。

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:48.290
<v Ira Glass>He asked if the patient was shot, because of the blood and  multiple wounds. The response was that  the patient was tasered. When the doctor  began to assess the patient, somebody mentioned that he was shot."
<v Ira Glass>他问病人是否中枪了，因为有血迹和多处伤口。得到的回答是病人被泰瑟枪电击了。当医生开始评估病人时，有人提到他中枪了。”

00:26:48.290 --> 00:27:05.760
<v Ira Glass>While Alan was recovering in the Intensive Care Unit, police handcuffed him to his bed and restricted his family's visits to  just 10 minutes a day, and they couldn't enter the room. They had to look at him through a window. That's because police saw him as a criminal. They charged Alan with two counts of aggravated assault, one for each officer.
<v Ira Glass>在艾伦于重症监护室康复期间，警察将他铐在床上，限制他家人的探视时间每天仅为10分钟，而且他们不能进入房间。他们不得不隔着窗户看他。这是因为警察把他视为罪犯。他们指控艾伦犯有两项加重攻击罪，每一名警官一项。

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:07.260
<v Alan Pean>I couldn't believe it.
<v Alan Pean>我简直不敢相信。

00:27:07.260 --> 00:27:08.750
<v Ira Glass>Again, here's Alan.
<v Ira Glass>艾伦再次说道。

00:27:08.750 --> 00:27:41.190
<v Alan Pean>I was expecting an apology. I was expecting 100% an apology. And that is not  what happened. And I could not believe it. I couldn't believe it. I don't know if you-- how is it that you have these officers who shoot an unarmed patient in the hospital, and then he is the bad guy, he's the guy who needs to go to jail? And it's jarring. I just don't understand.
<v Alan Pean>我当时在等一个道歉。我百分之百在等一个道歉。但这并没有发生。我简直不敢相信。我简直不敢相信。我不知道你——怎么会有这样的警察，在医院里枪击一个手无寸铁的病人，然后他反而是坏人，是需要坐牢的那个家伙？这很刺耳。我只是不明白。

00:27:41.190 --> 00:27:59.650
<v Ira Glass>Later, when federal investigators asked  Alan about the shooting, he said, quote, "He forgave the police officers. But what he cannot understand is why police officers were in his room, when he was an ill patient trying to get well."
<v Ira Glass>后来，当联邦调查人员就枪击案询问艾伦时，他说，引用，“他原谅了警察。但他无法理解的是，为什么警察会在他的病房里，当时他是一个生病的病人，试图康复。”

00:27:59.650 --> 00:28:19.110
<v Ira Glass>So what were armed police doing in a patient's room? This is something a New York Times correspondent, Elisabeth Rosenthal, has been looking into. As I said earlier, the story that you're hearing right now is a collaboration between our radio show and The New York Times . And when Elisabeth Rosenthal heard about Alan Pean, she wondered how common this kind of thing was.
<v Ira Glass>那么武装警察在病房里做什么？这是《纽约时报》记者伊丽莎白·罗森塔尔一直在调查的事情。正如我之前所说的，你现在听到的这个故事是我们广播节目与《纽约时报》合作完成的。当伊丽莎白·罗森塔尔听说艾伦·皮恩时，她想知道这种事情有多普遍。

00:28:19.110 --> 00:28:34.580
<v Ira Glass>Elisabeth is also a doctor and has worked  in hospitals, but none that allowed guns. And she just started calling hospitals and looking at the few surveys that exist on this stuff. The one national survey on this subject found that about half of all hospitals now allow guns.
<v Ira Glass>伊丽莎白也是一名医生，在医院工作过，但没有允许枪支的医院。她就开始给医院打电话，查看关于这方面仅有的几项调查。关于这一主题的一项全国调查发现，大约一半的医院现在允许带枪。

00:28:34.580 --> 00:28:39.570
<v Elisabeth Rosenthal>And the evidence suggests that that's about doubled in the past three years.
<v Elisabeth Rosenthal>有证据表明，这一数字在过去三年中翻了一番。

00:28:39.570 --> 00:29:02.230
<v Ira Glass>Doubled. Lots of hospitals now have police and ex-military doing security with guns. Hospitals aren't required to keep records on how many of their  patients are injured or killed by those guns. But Elisabeth looked for that as well by digging through old police blotters, court documents, news accounts, and surveys, and found over a dozen cases, most of them mentally ill patients.
<v Ira Glass>翻了一番。现在很多医院都有警察和前军人携带枪支进行安保。医院不需要记录有多少病人是被这些枪支伤害或杀害的。但伊丽莎白也通过挖掘旧的警察日志、法庭文件、新闻报道和调查寻找这些信息，发现了十几起案例，大多数是精神病患者。

00:29:02.230 --> 00:29:31.690
<v Elisabeth Rosenthal>First, on the very same day that Alan was shot, there was another psychiatric patient who was shot and killed at a hospital in suburban Cleveland. And then, just in January this year, there was another patient with bipolar illness who was shot and paralyzed by security in a Virginia hospital. And then sometimes people die when they're shocked with tasers. So there's two cases, one in Utah and one in Ohio, where patients were actually killed after repeated tasering.
<v Elisabeth Rosenthal>首先，在艾伦被枪击的同一天，另一名精神科病人在克利夫兰郊区的一家医院被枪杀。然后，就在今年1月，另一名患有双相情感障碍的病人在弗吉尼亚州的一家医院被安保人员枪击并瘫痪。有时候人们在被泰瑟枪电击时会死亡。所以有两起案例，一起在犹他州，一起在俄亥俄州，病人在多次泰瑟电击后死亡。

00:29:31.690 --> 00:29:43.040
<v Ira Glass>Both were by hospital security. Both were psychiatric patients. Of course, we all would hope that police would know how to deal with people who were mentally ill. They have to do it on the street all the time. But they don't always do it so well.
<v Ira Glass>都是由医院安保执行的。都是精神病患者。当然，我们都希望警察知道如何处理精神病患者。他们在街上总是不得不这样做。但他们并不总是做得那么好。

00:29:43.040 --> 00:30:05.830
<v Ira Glass>In December, The Washington Post published the results of a huge, yearlong investigation into police shootings nationwide. They found that last year, officers fatally shot at least 243 people with mental health problems. And in most of those cases, the cops who pulled the trigger hadn't received any training in how to deal with the mentally ill. And their tactics escalated the situation rather than calmed it down.
<v Ira Glass>去年12月，《华盛顿邮报》发布了一项针对全国警察枪击案的巨大、长达一年的调查结果。他们发现去年警察致命枪杀了至少243名有心理健康问题的人。在大多数情况下，扣动扳机的警察没有接受过任何关于如何处理精神病患者的培训。他们的战术升级了局势，而不是使其冷静下来。

00:30:05.830 --> 00:30:34.460
<v Ira Glass>So why are hospitals turning to police? Why have the number of guns in hospitals apparently doubled in just three years? Well, for one thing, violence in hospitals has also nearly doubled in that same period. It's up 40% according to statistics gathered by the International Health Care Security and Safety Foundation. Most of that violence is against hospital staff. A study last year by the Occupational Safety and Health Administration revealed that there is more violence in hospitals than any other workplace.
<v Ira Glass>那么为什么医院要求警察介入？为什么医院里的枪支数量在短短三年内明显翻了一番？嗯，一方面，医院内的暴力事件在此期间也几乎翻了一番。根据国际医疗安全与保障基金会收集的统计数据，上升了40%。大多数暴力事件是针对医护人员的。美国职业安全与健康管理局去年的一项研究显示，医院里的暴力事件比任何其他工作场所都要多。

00:30:34.460 --> 00:30:58.340
<v Ira Glass>And the way hospitals have responded to this violence, Elisabeth found, is a hodgepodge. One national survey found that half of all hospitals have tasers. They have them partly thanks to an aggressive marketing campaign directed at hospitals by TASER International. In New York's hospital system, which has several of the busiest emergency rooms in the country, security carries no weapons. And lots of hospitals, as I've said, have decided they're comfortable with guns.
<v Ira Glass>伊丽莎白发现，医院应对这种暴力的方式是一盘散沙。一项全国调查发现，半数医院拥有泰瑟枪。他们拥有这些枪支部分要归功于泰瑟国际公司针对医院进行的积极营销活动。在纽约医院系统，拥有全国最繁忙的几个急诊室，安保人员不携带武器。而正如我所说的，许多医院决定他们对枪支感到自在。

00:30:58.340 --> 00:31:07.890
<v Elisabeth Rosenthal>Someone said to me-- and I think this is true. It kind of reflects the divide in American society right now about guns, whether they make you feel safer or not.
<v Elisabeth Rosenthal>有人对我说——我认为这是真的。它某种程度上反映了美国社会目前关于枪支的鸿沟，无论它们是否让你感到更安全。

00:31:07.890 --> 00:31:09.900
<v Ira Glass>Right. And that just varies around the country, depending on--
<v Ira Glass>对。而这一点在全国范围内各不相同，取决于——

00:31:09.900 --> 00:31:29.570
<v Elisabeth Rosenthal>That varies a lot by your background, where you live in the country. Do you feel safer when you see a person with a gun at the door of the emergency room? I think the second issue, then, is if you decide you need weapons in your emergency room or in your hospital, how are the people trained who are using them?
<v Elisabeth Rosenthal>这一点在很大程度上取决于你的背景，你住在国家的什么地方。当你看到急诊室门口有一个带枪的人时，你感到更安全吗？我想第二个问题是，如果你决定在急诊室或医院里需要武器，那么使用武器的人受过什么样的训练？

00:31:29.570 --> 00:31:59.070
<v Ira Glass>This may actually be a bigger issue than the guns themselves. Have the people carrying the guns been trained to work in a health care setting? Because it's different. It's not like law enforcement. When there's a patient who's psychotic or acting up from mental illness, like Alan Pean, there are standard techniques that nurses and hospital security use to get control of the situation, non-confrontational ways of speaking to patients, restraints, medicines. Simply warning a patient that you're going to give them a shot can calm them down.
<v Ira Glass>这可能实际上是一个比枪支本身更大的问题。携带枪支的人受过在医疗环境中工作的训练吗？因为它是不同的。它不像执法。当有病人精神错乱或因精神疾病而行为不轨时，比如艾伦·皮恩，护士和医院安保使用标准技术来控制局势，比如非对抗性的方式与病人交谈、限制、药物。仅仅警告病人你要给他们打针就能让他们冷静下来。

00:31:59.070 --> 00:32:12.550
<v Ira Glass>And in her research, Elisabeth found some hospitals that taught these techniques to the security officers when they brought in tasers or guns and set clear rules about when to deploy weapons and how armed security would interact with staff and patients. But that definitely wasn't every place.
<v Ira Glass>在她的研究中，伊丽莎白发现一些医院在引入泰瑟枪或枪支时，对安保人员教授了这些技术，并制定了关于何时部署武器以及武装安保人员如何与工作人员和病人互动的明确规则。但这绝对不是每个地方都这样做。

00:32:12.550 --> 00:32:27.230
<v Elisabeth Rosenthal>I mean, that's the thing that shocked me. As I called around to different hospitals, there was no pattern to this. It was all over the map. And sometimes there had been a lot of thought put into the process. And sometimes, in fact, there seemed to be very little thought.
<v Elisabeth Rosenthal>我是说，那正是让我震惊的事情。当我打电话给不同的医院时，这没有模式。到处都是。有时人们对这个过程进行了深思熟虑。而有时，事实上，似乎考虑得很少。

00:32:27.230 --> 00:32:40.670
<v Ira Glass>When it comes to Alan Pean's case in St. Joseph Medical Center, the federal investigation notes that police officers were supposed to be trained to deescalate situations and restrain confused patients. But there's no evidence that it ever happened.
<v Ira Glass>谈到艾伦·皮恩在圣约瑟夫医学中心的案件，联邦调查指出，警察应该受过降级处理局势和限制困惑病人的训练。但没有证据表明这发生了。

00:32:40.670 --> 00:33:04.830
<v Ira Glass>This is quoting from the federal investigation report. Quote, "The facility failed to provide evidence that contracted police officers were trained in dealing with confused, disoriented, and aggressive patients. The facility failed to ensure contract police officers were trained in the use of safe, acceptable health care interventions for confused and aggressive patients. This resulted in the tasering, shooting, and handcuffing of a confused, aggressive patient." End quote.
<v Ira Glass>这是引用联邦调查报告。引用，“该机构未能提供证据证明签约警察受过处理困惑、迷失方向和具有攻击性的病人的培训。该机构未能确保签约警察受过在困惑和具有攻击性的病人身上使用安全、可接受的医疗干预措施的训练。这导致了一名困惑、具有攻击性的病人被泰瑟枪电击、枪击和铐住。”引用结束。

00:33:04.830 --> 00:33:18.540
<v Ira Glass>This is actually what was galling to the staffer that I talked to from St. Joseph Medical Center. This person said that these cops were walking into a totally normal situation for the hospital that any trained staff or the regular hospital security could have handled without firearms.
<v Ira Glass>这实际上是让我交谈的那名来自圣约瑟夫医学中心的员工感到气愤的地方。这个人说，这些警察进入的是医院完全正常的情况，任何受过训练的工作人员或常规医院安保都可以不带武器解决。

00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:33.740
<v Scott Shepherd>That's an everyday thing for us. We see that all the time. You see it all day. You see confused, demented people that can be very combative. I mean, hospital staff, we get abused and we're put in more danger than-- or just as much danger as cops on the street.
<v Scott Shepherd>那对我们来说是每天都在发生的事情。我们经常看到这种情况。你整天都在看。你看到困惑、痴呆的人，他们可能非常有攻击性。我是说，医院员工，我们受到虐待，我们处于比——或者和街上的警察面临一样大的危险。

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:51.690
<v Scott Shepherd>We don't walk around with guns. We get hit. We get hit by patients all the time. As long as that person doesn't have a weapon to harm someone else, why should you have a weapon? Or why should you use your weapon?
<v Scott Shepherd>我们没有带着枪走动。我们被打。病人总是打我们。只要那个人没有武器去伤害别人，为什么你应该有武器？或者为什么你应该使用你的武器？

00:33:51.690 --> 00:34:08.510
<v Ira Glass>To be clear, this medical staffer thought that it was totally appropriate. They liked it. It made them feel safer that a couple of cops with guns would be in the emergency room in case somebody with a weapon came into the hospital. But sending cops to the room of somebody like Alan Pean? That's totally different.
<v Ira Glass>需要明确的是，这名医护人员认为那是完全适当的。他们喜欢那样。有几个带枪的警察在急诊室以防带武器的人进入医院，这让他们感到更安全。但派警察去艾伦·皮恩那样的人的病房？那完全是另一回事。

00:34:08.510 --> 00:34:23.250
<v Scott Shepherd>First of all, if someone's delusional and two cops walk into the room, it's a normal reaction for that patient that's having delusions, or that psychotic, to react violently. That's how they are. That's what they do.
<v Scott Shepherd>首先，如果某人有妄想症，两个警察走进房间，对那个有妄想症或精神病的人来说，反应具有暴力倾向是一个正常的反应。他们就是那样。他们就是这么做的。

00:34:23.250 --> 00:34:34.230
<v Scott Shepherd>So you kind of give that space. You stay back. You find out what's going on. You try and talk to them to see if they're safe to approach or not. And that's what-- I'm not sure if that was done or not.
<v Scott Shepherd>所以你给那个空间。你退后。你找出发生了什么。你尝试跟他们交谈，看看他们是否安全可以靠近。而那正是——我不确定这是否被做了。

00:34:34.230 --> 00:34:50.310
<v Ira Glass>To paraphrase an old saying, guns in hospitals don't kill people. Guns in hospitals wielded by people who are not trained to have guns in hospitals kill people.
<v Ira Glass>借用一句古老的话来说，医院里的枪支不会杀人。在医院里由没有受过在医院使用枪支训练的人挥舞枪支才会杀人。

00:34:50.310 --> 00:35:03.330
<v Ira Glass>So better training of the officers in Alan Pean's case might have prevented the shooting. But in this particular case, there was something else that could have stopped this from happening, something simple. Alan could have been given a psychiatric evaluation.
<v Ira Glass>所以对艾伦·皮恩案件中的警察进行更好的训练可能阻止了枪击。但在这种特定情况下，还有别的事情可能阻止这件事发生，很简单的事情。艾伦本可以接受精神病评估。

00:35:03.330 --> 00:35:19.080
<v Ira Glass>Remember, the ER staff noted Alan's erratic mental state in his chart but didn't get him evaluated. And Alan's father asked for a psych evaluation, but he was ignored. The St. Joseph staffer that I talked to said it's hard to understand why.
<v Ira Glass>记住，急诊室工作人员在图表中记录了艾伦不稳定的精神状态，但没有让他接受评估。艾伦的父亲要求进行心理评估，但他被无视了。我交谈的那名圣约瑟夫员工说很难理解为什么。

00:35:19.080 --> 00:35:40.710
<v Scott Shepherd>I hate to say it because we never want to talk bad about anyone that we work with-- and I wasn't there, but if that was said to a medical professional, then their wishes should have been complied with immediately. That's the protocol. That's what's supposed to happen.
<v Scott Shepherd>我不喜欢这么说，因为我们永远不想说我们一起工作的任何人的坏话——我当时不在场，但如果那是对医疗专业人员说的，那么他们的意愿就应该立即得到满足。那是规程。那是应该发生的事。

00:35:40.710 --> 00:35:46.660
<v Ira Glass>And is it true that if he had been diagnosed as a psych patient, he would have been in a psych ward where no guns are allowed?
<v Ira Glass>是真的吗，如果他被诊断为精神病病人，他会被安置在精神病房，那里不允许带枪？

00:35:46.660 --> 00:35:48.070
<v Scott Shepherd>Correct.
<v Scott Shepherd>正确。

00:35:48.070 --> 00:35:50.240
<v Ira Glass>So that would've prevented this whole thing?
<v Ira Glass>所以那本会阻止整件事？

00:35:50.240 --> 00:35:51.760
<v Scott Shepherd>Correct.
<v Scott Shepherd>正确。

00:35:51.760 --> 00:36:00.890
<v Ira Glass>You don't want guns in psychiatry wards for common sense reasons. It's too dangerous. You don't want a patient to grab the gun. And seeing firearms might agitate certain patients.
<v Ira Glass>出于常识，你不希望精神病房里有枪。太危险了。你不希望病人抢枪。而且看到枪支可能会激怒某些病人。

00:36:00.890 --> 00:36:28.980
<v Ira Glass>Because of that, almost universally guns are banned from state mental hospitals in the United States. When officers enter those facilities, they check their guns at the door. Possibly the one exception is Texas, where Alan was shot, where just last month on January 1, four months after Alan's shooting, it became legal to carry licensed firearms in state-run psychiatric hospitals.
<v Ira Glass>正因为如此，美国几乎普遍禁止在州立精神病院携带枪支。当警察进入这些设施时，他们会在门口检查枪支。可能的一个例外是德克萨斯州，即艾伦被枪击的地方，就在上个月的1月1日，即艾伦枪击案发生四个月后，在州立精神病院携带合法枪支变得合法了。

00:36:28.980 --> 00:36:56.610
<v Ira Glass>When the Pean family filed a complaint against the hospital with the federal government, specifically the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, or CMS, CMS felt the shooting raised serious enough questions about St. Joseph Medical Center that they had investigators at the hospital within five days of the shooting and publicly raised the possibility that St. Joseph might lose its federal funding. When the investigators interviewed St. Joseph's Chief Executive Officer Mark Bernard about the incident, he told them that he stood by the officers.
<v Ira Glass>当皮恩一家向联邦政府，特别是医疗保险和医疗补助服务中心，即CMS，投诉该医院时，CMS认为枪击事件引发了关于圣约瑟夫医学中心的足够严重的问题，以至于他们在枪击事件发生后的五天内就在医院有了调查人员，并公开提出了圣约瑟夫可能会失去联邦资金的可能性。当调查人员采访圣约瑟夫的首席执行官马克·伯纳德关于此事时，他告诉他们，他支持这些警察。

00:36:56.610 --> 00:37:19.330
<v Ira Glass>This from the report, he said, quote, that "the minute the patient hit the uniformed police officer, it became a criminal offense, and the officers went into police mode and were justified in the actions they took to protect themselves." When asked what other interventions could have been used by the security staff, the CEO said, quote, "if it should happen today, they would not have done anything different."
<v Ira Glass>来自报告，他说，引用，病人击中身穿制服的警察的那一刻，这就变成了刑事犯罪，警察进入了警察模式，他们为保护自己所采取的行动是合理的。当被问到安保人员可以使用什么其他干预措施时，首席执行官说，引用，“如果今天发生同样的事情，他们不会做任何不同的处理。”

00:37:19.330 --> 00:37:41.070
<v Ira Glass>I wondered if the CEO still felt that way, given the evidence in the federal report that police officers may not have been properly trained to deal with a patient like Alan Pean and that hospital staff failed to do a psychiatric evaluation. The hospital referred this and all of our questions to an outside crisis manager they work with named Rhonda Barnat. I was told that CEO Mark Bernard declined to answer that question.
<v Ira Glass>我不知道鉴于联邦报告中警察可能没有受过妥善训练来处理像艾伦·皮恩这样的病人，且医院工作人员未能进行精神病评估的证据，首席执行官是否仍然有这种感觉。医院将这个问题以及我们所有的提问交给了他们合作的一位外部危机经理，名字叫朗达·巴纳特。我被告知首席执行官马克·伯纳德拒绝回答这个问题。

00:37:41.070 --> 00:37:55.630
<v Ira Glass>I ran CEO Mark Bernard's statement, quote, "If it should happen today, they would not have done anything different," by Alan's father. Remember, as a physician and familiar with hospitals and how they work, his response is that of a doctor and a father.
<v Ira Glass>我把首席执行官马克·伯纳德的声明，引用，“如果今天发生同样的事情，他们不会做任何不同的处理，”告诉了艾伦的父亲。记住，作为一名医生，熟悉医院及其运作方式，他的回应是一位医生和一位父亲的回应。

00:37:55.630 --> 00:38:20.390
<v Dr. Harold Pean>That's crazy. That's totally crazy. Can you believe that? So he didn't see anything wrong. He said that was normal procedure. I mean, can you believe a CEO saying that? That's terrible. I don't understand it. So that would be standard procedure for a patient who's sick, instead of sending their doctor, send police officers and shoot them. That's totally absurd.
<v Dr. Harold Pean>那太疯狂了。那完全疯狂。你能相信吗？所以他没觉得有什么不对。他说那是标准程序。我是说，你能相信首席执行官这么说吗？那太可怕了。我不明白。所以这就是对待生病病人的标准程序，不派医生，却派警察并射杀他们。这完全荒谬。

00:38:20.390 --> 00:38:48.710
<v Ira Glass>We sent what Alan's dad said to the hospital's crisis manager to give CEO Mark Bernard a chance to respond, along with a long list of other questions. In reply, the hospital issued this statement, which it said came from CEO Mark Bernard. Quote, "In the wake of this sad event, we are reviewing our practices and procedures as we continue to provide the best possible care to those we serve."
<v Ira Glass>我们把艾伦父亲所说的发送给了医院的危机经理，给首席执行官马克·伯纳德一个回应的机会，以及一份长长的其他提问清单。作为回复，医院发表了这份声明，称其来自首席执行官马克·伯纳德。引用，“在这场悲惨事件之后，我们正在审查我们的实践和程序，因为我们继续为我们服务的人提供尽可能最好的护理。”

00:38:48.710 --> 00:39:15.170
<v Ira Glass>Change is happening at St. Joseph. According to the report by federal investigators, St. Joseph is now limited where police officers can go in the hospital to peripheral areas. They can't go to patients' rooms unless staff has tried everything else to calm a situation. St. Joseph also has to do crisis prevention training with staff and police. They have 17 months to get their act together, or they lose their federal funding.
<v Ira Glass>圣约瑟夫正在发生变化。根据联邦调查人员的报告，圣约瑟夫现在限制了警察可以在医院里去的地方，只能去外围区域。除非工作人员已经尝试了其他所有方法来使局势冷静下来，否则他们不能去病房。圣约瑟夫还必须对工作人员和警察进行危机预防培训。他们有17个月的时间来纠正错误，否则他们将失去联邦资金。

00:39:15.170 --> 00:39:27.710
<v Ira Glass>OK. So basically, you drove yourself to a hospital. And when you're at the hospital, you said explicitly, you're having a manic episode. You're unarmed. And you end up shot.
<v Ira Glass>好的。所以基本上，你自己开车去了医院。当你在医院时，你明确说，你正在经历躁狂发作。你手无寸铁。最后你却中枪了。

00:39:27.710 --> 00:39:30.480
<v Alan Pean>Yep. Correct.
<v Alan Pean>是的。没错。

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:36.430
<v Ira Glass>OK. I don't even know what my question is. I just want to say, that seems bad.
<v Ira Glass>好的。我甚至不知道我的问题是什么。我只是想说，那看起来很糟糕。

00:39:36.430 --> 00:39:46.230
<v Alan Pean>On a whole bunch of levels. And that opened my eyes in a way that I sort of never want it to.
<v Alan Pean>在很多层面上。那让我大开眼界，以一种我不希望的方式。

00:39:46.230 --> 00:39:49.160
<v Ira Glass>What do you mean?
<v Ira Glass>你是什么意思？

00:39:49.160 --> 00:39:52.240
<v Alan Pean>Well, I'm black.
<v Alan Pean>嗯，我是黑人。

00:39:52.240 --> 00:39:54.920
<v Ira Glass>Because you don't think this would have happened if you were white?
<v Ira Glass>因为你不认为如果你是白人这会发生？

00:39:54.920 --> 00:39:56.100
<v Alan Pean>No, I don't.
<v Alan Pean>不，我不认为。

00:39:56.100 --> 00:39:56.910
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:39:56.910 --> 00:40:12.340
<v Alan Pean>That the only reason this guy was able to look at me and decide that, hey, he needs to be shot in the chest. I think they just-- another black guy acting crazy. Angry black guy.
<v Alan Pean>那个人能够看着我并决定，嘿，他需要被射中胸部，唯一的原因就是我是黑人。我想他们只是——又一个发疯的黑人。愤怒的黑人。

00:40:12.340 --> 00:40:25.970
<v Ira Glass>In the immediate wake of the shooting, Alan's father says that one of the questions that police asked him over and over was, did Alan have a criminal record? Alan told me that, of course, he's seen all the news stories about young black men being shot by police.
<v Ira Glass>在枪击事件发生后，艾伦的父亲说警察反复问他的一个问题是，艾伦有犯罪记录吗？艾伦告诉我，当然，他看过所有关于年轻黑人被警察枪杀的新闻报道。

00:40:25.970 --> 00:40:36.910
<v Alan Pean>I would think outrage. It's an outrage. It's a complete outrage. But I would never think that it would happen to me. I'm privileged, right? I acknowledge that. I acknowledge I'm privileged.
<v Alan Pean>我会认为是愤慨。这是愤怒。这是完全的愤怒。但我从未想过这会发生在我身上。我有特权，对吧？我承认这一点。我承认我有特权。

00:40:36.910 --> 00:40:43.880
<v Ira Glass>This is a family of doctors, a family with money. That car that he crashed into the hospital was a 2010 Lexus.
<v Ira Glass>这是一个医生家庭，一个有钱的家庭。他撞进医院的那辆车是2010年的雷克萨斯。

00:40:43.880 --> 00:41:19.470
<v Ira Glass>And it's interesting. Of the four black men in the family, three of them thought that they were living a life where they would never have this kind of run-in with a cop. One of Alan's closest friends is a cop. His dad is friends with cops. Only Christian, the oldest son, thought it could happen to him. In fact, when his father texted him the day of the shooting to say that something had happened-- Alan was in the ICU, they didn't know what happened yet-- Christian's first text back was, wait, what? What? Did they shoot him?
<v Ira Glass>很有趣。在家庭里的四名黑人男性中，三个人认为他们过着永远不会与警察发生这种冲突的生活。艾伦最亲密的朋友之一就是警察。他父亲和警察是朋友。只有克里斯蒂安，最大的儿子，认为这可能发生在他身上。事实上，当他父亲在枪击那天给他发短信说发生了什么事——艾伦在重症监护室，他们还不知道发生了什么——克里斯蒂安回的第一条短信是，等等，什么？什么？他们枪击他了吗？

00:41:19.470 --> 00:41:28.600
<v Ira Glass>Alan Pean nearly died. The bullet hit him squarely in the chest. It missed his aorta by millimeters. He lost a third of the blood in his body.
<v Ira Glass>艾伦·皮恩差点死了。子弹直中他的胸部。距离主动脉只有几毫米。他失去了身体三分之一的血液。

00:41:28.600 --> 00:41:46.380
<v Alan Pean>I can't believe that I'm even here, walking around, standing. And I don't know why he would shoot me in the chest unless he was trying to kill me. That's a kill shot, so that's-- I don't think too much about it. I try not to, I think, actually, honestly.
<v Alan Pean>我简直不敢相信我还在这里，走来走去，站着。而且我不知道他为什么要射中我的胸部，除非他想杀我。那是致命一枪，所以那是——我对此想得不多。我想我尽量不去想，实际上，老实说。

00:41:46.380 --> 00:41:47.930
<v Ira Glass>Because it just freaked you out?
<v Ira Glass>因为那吓到你了？

00:41:47.930 --> 00:41:48.690
<v Alan Pean>It does.
<v Alan Pean>确实。

00:41:48.690 --> 00:41:50.660
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:41:50.660 --> 00:42:07.720
<v Alan Pean>And I really can't handle it sometimes. Well, I can take it. I heard you say it like that. It's just, I still think I'm in shock, that I don't know how to handle it. I don't know how to handle it.
<v Alan Pean>而且有时我真的无法处理。好吧，我能承受。我听到你这么说。只是，我觉得我仍然处于震惊中，我不懂得怎么去处理它。我不懂得怎么去处理它。

00:42:07.720 --> 00:42:33.670
<v Ira Glass>Well, yeah. That day in August, Alan had a delusion that people were out to kill him, that if he walked out of his apartment, he'd be shot. There were assassins after him. He was going to get shot. And then, he was shot. This literally crazy thought came true. How do you make sense of that?
<v Ira Glass>嗯，是的。八月的那一天，艾伦有一种妄想，认为人们想杀他，如果他走出公寓，他会被枪杀。有杀手在追他。他会中枪。然后，他确实中枪了。这个完全疯狂的想法成真了。你怎么去理解这一点？

00:42:33.670 --> 00:42:46.310
<v Ira Glass>The New York Times ' version of this story is online at their website. It's great, with great photos, at nytimes.com/hospital. Since we first aired this story in 2016, the assault charges against Alan Pean were dismissed by a grand jury. Another grand jury looked into the behavior of the two officers involved in this shooting and did not indict them. The Texas State Legislature is currently considering a new bill that would allow state-run psychiatric hospitals to ban guns again.
<v Ira Glass>《纽约时报》关于这个故事的版本在他们的网站上。很棒，配有很棒的照片，在nytimes.com/hospital。自从我们在2016年首次播出这个故事以来，对艾伦·皮恩的攻击指控被大陪审团驳回了。另一个大陪审团调查了参与这次枪击案的两名警察的行为，并没有起诉他们。德克萨斯州立法机构目前正在考虑一项新法案，将再次允许州立精神病院禁止枪支。

00:42:46.310 --> 00:43:00.470
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, consciously telling yourself things that you know are not true as a way to use your mind and its power. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，有意识地告诉自己一些你知道不真实的事情，作为一种利用你的大脑及其力量的方式。一分钟后，从芝加哥公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:43:00.470 --> 00:43:04.240
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, "My Damn Mind."
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目主题是“我该死的大脑”。

00:43:04.240 --> 00:43:26.100
<v Ira Glass>Our first act was about somebody whose mind was sending him jumping off of balconies. And now, in this part of our show, we're turning to a very different story-- still about a man and his brain and the awesome and confounding power of one's own mind. In this case, though, it is about a man harnessing that power for a constructive purpose of his own. This is Act Two of our show. Act Two, "Don't Need to Know Basis."
<v Ira Glass>我们的第一幕是关于某人的大脑让他从阳台上跳下来。现在，在节目的这一部分，我们转向一个截然不同的故事——仍然是关于一个男人和他的大脑，以及他自身大脑那种令人敬畏和困惑的力量。不过在这种情况下，它是关于一个人为了自己建设性的目的利用那种力量。这是我们节目的第二幕。第二幕，“不需要知道的依据”。

00:43:26.100 --> 00:43:42.360
<v Ira Glass>The man in this story is Michael Kinsley-- writer, editor, known for, among other things, starting the online magazine Slate and for being just a very sharp, funny observer of American politics and human nature generally, including his own nature. He is a human. Nancy Updike talked to him.
<v Ira Glass>这个故事中的男人是迈克尔·金斯利——作家、编辑，以创办在线杂志《Slate》以及作为美国政治和人类本性，包括他自己本性，非常敏锐、有趣的观察者而闻名。他是一个人。南希·厄普代克采访了他。

00:43:42.360 --> 00:44:21.380
<v Nancy Updike>Michael Kinsley wrote an essay years ago-- it was funny, naturally-- in which he announced that he had Parkinson's Disease. The essay was called "In Defense of Denial," because what he was really announcing was that he had already had Parkinson's for the previous eight years. In that time, he said, the way he had chosen to use his mind, his deliberate strategy for dealing with Parkinson's, had been denial. And he was not writing to confess or apologize or to vow not to engage in denial in the future. He was talking about the benefits of self-deception and secrecy.
<v Nancy Updike>迈克尔·金斯利多年前写过一篇文章——自然很有趣——在文章中他宣布自己患有帕金森病。文章标题是“为否认辩护”，因为他真正宣布的是，他在此前的八年里就已经患有帕金森病了。他说，在那段时间里，他选择使用大脑的方式，他应对帕金森病的刻意策略，一直是否认。他不是为了忏悔或道歉，或者发誓未来不再进行否认而写作。他谈论的是自我欺骗和保密的益处。

00:44:21.380 --> 00:44:42.870
<v Nancy Updike>His argument was, when it comes to serious illness, like with all terrible things that happen to us, sure, yes, the goal is to get to acceptance-- eventually, if you can. But acceptance is not a strategy, he said. It's not something you can just do. He said, really, there are only two strategies, denial or confrontation.
<v Nancy Updike>他的论点是，当涉及到严重的疾病时，就像发生在我们身上所有的可怕事情一样，当然，是的，目标是最终实现接受——如果你能的话。但他说是说接受不是一种策略。这不是你可以简单做到的。他说，实际上，只有两种策略，否认或对抗。

00:44:42.870 --> 00:45:11.980
<v Michael  Kinsley>Confrontation, it's the strategy that everything in our culture pushes you toward, fighting it all the way. And also joining the community of sufferers of whatever, making it your life. Who are you? I'm someone with Parkinson's. And I'm going off to my support group.
<v Michael  Kinsley>对抗，这是我们文化中一切都推动你走向的策略，一直与之斗争。而且还要加入无论什么的受害者社区，将其作为你的生活。你是谁？我是一个患有帕金森病的人。我正要去参加我的互助小组。

00:45:11.980 --> 00:45:14.040
<v Nancy Updike>Right. They really engage.
<v Nancy Updike>对。他们确实很投入。

00:45:14.040 --> 00:45:34.050
<v Michael  Kinsley>Yes. Michael J. Fox is an example, and a thoroughly admirable example of how to react. He's made it his cause, both his public cause and his personal cause. And that's great. But I just decided, that is not for me.
<v Michael  Kinsley>是的。迈克尔·J·福克斯是一个例子，一个彻底令人钦佩的关于如何应对的例子。他将其作为自己的事业，既是公共事业也是个人事业。那很好。但我只是决定，那不适合我。

00:45:34.050 --> 00:45:35.520
<v Nancy Updike>You went with denial.
<v Nancy Updike>你选择了否认。

00:45:35.520 --> 00:46:00.360
<v Michael  Kinsley>Denial, which is the opposite. You don't think about it. You don't get involved. You don't learn everything you can about it. You try to live your life and spend your time outside of that world. And in many ways, this was a cowardly choice. But it was the choice I made.
<v Michael  Kinsley>否认，这是相反的。你不去想它。你不参与其中。你不去尽可能多地了解它。你试图过你的生活，把时间花在那个世界之外。在很多方面，这是一个懦弱的选择。但这是我做出的选择。

00:46:00.360 --> 00:46:13.880
<v Nancy Updike>I really appreciate a person who's prepared to use every mental trick in dealing with sickness. Mike's essay spoke to me. I have experienced the clear benefits of denial. I know what it's like to choose that option.
<v Nancy Updike>我真的很欣赏一个准备好在处理疾病时使用每一个心理技巧的人。迈克的话触动了我。我已经体验到了否认的明显好处。我知道选择那个选项是什么感觉。

00:46:13.880 --> 00:46:47.230
<v Nancy Updike>I had a tumor in my abdomen three years ago-- cancer. In the week before the surgery, denial-- carefully executed not thinking-- was my only goal. I wasn't graceful or even logical about it. For some reason, my denial involved making several trips to the drugstore, Duane Reade, to buy dental floss, and yet more dental floss. Not sure why floss. Mike suggested maybe I was stockpiling a message to myself. You will live to floss long into the future.
<v Nancy Updike>三年前我的腹部有一个肿瘤——癌症。在手术前的一周，否认——精心执行的不去想——是我唯一的目标。我对此既不优雅也不合逻辑。出于某种原因，我的否认涉及多次去药店，Duane Reade，去买牙线，以及更多更多的牙线。不确定为什么牙线。迈克建议也许我是在囤积一个留给自己的信息。你将活得很久，并一直使用牙线。

00:46:47.230 --> 00:47:11.720
<v Nancy Updike>When he chose denial, Mike did feel bad about deceiving other people, not just the general public but also people he knew. And he said he tried not to lie outright. But denial was very good to him. It gave him eight years of privacy, eight years of relative normalcy. Nothing else could have done that. And he saw another benefit.
<v Nancy Updike>当他选择否认时，迈克确实对欺骗其他人感到难过，不仅是大众，还有他认识的人。他说他尽量不直接撒谎。但否认对他非常好。它给了他八年的隐私，八年的相对正常。没有其他东西能做到这一点。他又看到了另一个好处。

00:47:11.720 --> 00:47:34.170
<v Michael  Kinsley>If you're lucky, you have good friends and close relatives who are going to help you get through it. But the best person in the world's sympathy is going to be limited. So you do think, well, do I want to use that up?
<v Michael  Kinsley>如果你幸运的话，你有好朋友和近亲会帮助你度过难关。但世界上最好的人的同情心也是有限的。所以你确实会想，好吧，我想要用完它吗？

00:47:34.170 --> 00:47:46.340
<v Michael  Kinsley>Maybe that's a crazy way to look at it. But I thought, I'm going to need their sympathy in years to come. And I don't especially need it now.
<v Michael  Kinsley>也许那是一种疯狂的看法。但我想，在未来的岁月里我将需要他们的同情。而我现在并不特别需要它。

00:47:46.340 --> 00:48:32.300
<v Nancy Updike>As Mike's symptoms progressed, bit by bit, some tremors, some stiffness, he told more people, bit by bit. And he ignored each person's advice in turn because every person was saying the same thing-- no more denial. The upshot was that Mike continued not reading up on Parkinson's, continued trying to know as little as possible, while his friends were reading up. Thanks to a couple of them, he ended up at the Cleveland Clinic to get brain surgery, basically because his friends had been badgering him to go there and see a doctor who was surgically placing electrodes in the brains of people with Parkinson's to help slow down the progress of the disease's physical symptoms.
<v Nancy Updike>随着迈克的症状逐渐加重，一些颤抖，一些僵硬，他告诉了更多人，一点一点。他反过来忽略了每个人的建议，因为每个人都在说同样的话——不要再否认了。结果是迈克继续不去阅读关于帕金森病的东西，继续试图知道得越少越好，而他的朋友们正在阅读。多亏了其中几个，他最终去了克利夫兰诊所接受脑部手术，基本上是因为他的朋友一直缠着他去那里看一位医生，那位医生正在手术地将电极植入帕金森病人的大脑中，以帮助减缓疾病的物理症状进程。

00:48:32.300 --> 00:48:38.160
<v Michael  Kinsley>Well, it was sort of a slippery slope. They said, why don't you just go and let him examine you and see if you're suitable for it.
<v Michael  Kinsley>嗯，那是一个滑坡。他们说，你为什么不去让他检查一下，看看你是否适合它。

00:48:38.160 --> 00:48:40.920
<v Nancy Updike>It's like a blind date. Maybe just go meet her. She's nice.
<v Nancy Updike>就像相亲。也许只是去见见她。她很好。

00:48:40.920 --> 00:49:00.240
<v Michael  Kinsley>Yeah. Yes. Well, I did. And they wanted to make sure you were up for this. And after I had been to Cleveland two or three times, they suddenly said, well, how about July 12? I'm making up the date. But they--
<v Michael  Kinsley>是的。是的。嗯，我去了。他们想确保你准备好做这个。在我去过克利夫兰两三次后，他们突然说，7月12日怎么样？我编造了这个日期。但他们——

00:49:00.240 --> 00:49:02.440
<v Nancy Updike>Wow. They got very specific all of a sudden.
<v Nancy Updike>哇。他们突然变得非常具体。

00:49:02.440 --> 00:49:35.240
<v Michael  Kinsley>Yeah. And I didn't think about it nearly as long as I should have. There is a controversy about which particular spot in the brain you should go to. And different surgeons do different spots. But that's a perfect example of something I didn't research but that many people would and do. But I said, OK. And the next thing I knew, I was in Cleveland.
<v Michael  Kinsley>是的。我考虑的时间远没有我应该考虑的那么长。关于你应该去大脑中的哪个特定点存在争议。不同的外科医生做不同的点。但那是一个我没有研究但许多人会研究并且确实研究过的东西的完美例子。但我说，好的。我还没反应过来，我就在克利夫兰了。

00:49:35.240 --> 00:49:38.060
<v Nancy Updike>And were you nervous?
<v Nancy Updike>你紧张吗？

00:49:38.060 --> 00:50:01.390
<v Michael  Kinsley>Yeah. I mean, I'm not that good at denial that I wouldn't be nervous about brain surgery. So they just-- they said we're just going to make a little hole. And they said it's just dime-sized. And that took me back, because I think of a dime-sized hole as being pretty big.
<v Michael  Kinsley>是的。我是说，我不擅长否认到连脑部手术都不会紧张。所以他们只是——他们说我们只是要挖一个小洞。他们说那只有一角硬币大小。那让我退缩了，因为我认为一角硬币大小的洞相当大。

00:50:01.390 --> 00:50:06.040
<v Nancy Updike>Large. For them it's small. For you, it's big. Because it's your brain.
<v Nancy Updike>大。对他们来说是小的。对你来说，很大。因为那是你的大脑。

00:50:06.040 --> 00:50:12.530
<v Michael  Kinsley>Yeah. But I thought, well, this is how they do it.
<v Michael  Kinsley>是的。但我心想，嗯，这就是他们的做法。

00:50:12.530 --> 00:50:21.370
<v Nancy Updike>OK. And they screw your head to the table so it won't move while the surgery is happening right? Even though it's general anesthesia, it's not local or anything.
<v Nancy Updike>好的。他们把你的头拧在桌子上，这样在手术发生时它就不会动，对吧？尽管是全身麻醉，不是局部麻醉什么的。

00:50:21.370 --> 00:50:23.710
<v Michael  Kinsley>No. No, it's not general anesthesia.
<v Michael  Kinsley>不。不，不是全身麻醉。

00:50:23.710 --> 00:50:26.520
<v Nancy Updike>You were awake?
<v Nancy Updike>你是清醒的？

00:50:26.520 --> 00:50:27.790
<v Michael  Kinsley>For most of the time.
<v Michael  Kinsley>大部分时间。

00:50:27.790 --> 00:50:29.470
<v Nancy Updike>Oh, my god.
<v Nancy Updike>哦，我的天。

00:50:29.470 --> 00:50:43.360
<v Michael  Kinsley>Because they had to-- well, it's like you're down in the basement trying to replace a fuse. And someone has to go upstairs to see if the light comes on. And so that was me.
<v Michael  Kinsley>因为他们必须——嗯，就像你在地下室试图更换保险丝。必须有人上楼看看灯是否亮了。所以那个人是我。

00:50:43.360 --> 00:50:46.560
<v Nancy Updike>Right. You were the test about whether they were getting it right.
<v Nancy Updike>对。你是关于他们是否做得对的测试。

00:50:46.560 --> 00:50:58.320
<v Michael  Kinsley>Yeah. And it was quite weird. They could press a button or something, and my hand would start to wave.
<v Michael  Kinsley>是的。而且非常奇怪。他们可以按一个按钮什么的，我的手就会开始挥动。

00:50:58.320 --> 00:51:12.250
<v Nancy Updike>The surgery did what it was supposed to. It slowed the progress of the physical symptoms which then allowed Mike to continue his project of exercising however much denial he possibly could at every stage.
<v Nancy Updike>手术做了它应该做的事。它减缓了物理症状的进展，然后允许迈克继续他试图在每一个阶段都尽可能多地行使否认的项目。

00:51:12.250 --> 00:51:25.490
<v Nancy Updike>Parkinson's isn't usually fatal. Or, as Mike has cheerfully written over the years, Parkinson's gives you a decent chance for something else to get you first. But Parkinson's does get worse over time.
<v Nancy Updike>帕金森病通常不是致命的。或者，正如迈克多年来愉快地写道，帕金森病给你一个体面的机会让别的东西先夺走你。但帕金森病确实会随着时间推移而恶化。

00:51:25.490 --> 00:51:50.290
<v Nancy Updike>And maybe even more galling, Parkinson's is not just one thing. Eight years after the brain surgery, Mike wrote another essay about Parkinson's in The New Yorker . It's called "Have You Lost Your Mind?" Look it up if you haven't read it. It's great. It's about the research, more and more of it over the last couple of decades, indicating that Parkinson's can include cognitive symptoms. It can affect your thinking.
<v Nancy Updike>也许更让人恼火的是，帕金森病不仅仅是一件事。脑部手术八年后，迈克在《纽约客》上写了另一篇关于帕金森病的文章。标题是“你失去理智了吗？”如果你还没读过，去看看。很棒。它是关于过去几十年来越来越多的研究，表明帕金森病可能包括认知症状。它会影响你的思维。

00:51:50.290 --> 00:52:05.220
<v Nancy Updike>A doctor once said to you when you asked him-- I think it was a him-- when you asked him about the cognitive issues associated with Parkinson's, he said, well, you may lose your edge.
<v Nancy Updike>一位医生曾经在你问他时对你说——我想是位他——当你问他关于与帕金森病相关的认知问题时，他说，嗯，你可能会失去优势。

00:52:05.220 --> 00:52:27.850
<v Michael  Kinsley>Yes. That was the most terrifying moment, I think, in the whole process. I mean, I make my living off of my edge. And also, it's part of my sense of who I am.
<v Michael  Kinsley>是的。那是我认为整个过程中最可怕的时刻。我是说，我靠我的优势为生。而且这也是我对自己是谁的感知的一部分。

00:52:27.850 --> 00:52:48.830
<v Nancy Updike>He thought he was sort of soft-pedaling something, saying, oh, you might lose your edge, as though you're still an intact thing with just a slightly softer edge. And meanwhile, for you, you were thinking, but I am my edge. My edge is me. I'm not a thing with an edge. The edge is-- that's me. That's what feels like me.
<v Nancy Updike>他以为他是在某种程度上软化了什么，说，哦，你可能会失去优势，就好像你仍然是一个完整的东西，只是优势稍微软化了一点。而同时，对你来说，你在想，但我就是我的优势。我的优势就是我。我不是一个有优势的东西。优势是——那就是我。那才是让我感觉是我自己的东西。

00:52:48.830 --> 00:53:01.330
<v Michael  Kinsley>Yeah, that's exactly it. But let me say to anyone who cares who's listening that I have not lost my mind.
<v Michael  Kinsley>是的，正是那样。但让我对任何正在倾听并在意的人说，我没有失去理智。

00:53:01.330 --> 00:53:24.430
<v Nancy Updike>Mike says the rallying cry for his generation is death before dementia, only after a very long life, of course. He wrote in his New Yorker essay that the kind of thing you'd want people to say about you, looking back, would be something like, "he was 102 years old when he was accidentally shot by a neighbor and still sharp as a tack."
<v Nancy Updike>迈克说他这一代人的战斗口号是痴呆前死亡，当然是在非常长寿之后。他在《纽约客》文章中写道，你会想要人们对你评价的那种事情，回顾过去，会是像这样的，“他102岁时被邻居意外枪杀，依然思维敏捷。”

00:53:24.430 --> 00:53:43.420
<v Nancy Updike>Parkinson's is different from Alzheimer's. The cognitive issues vary a lot from person to person, and they're still being researched. But dementia is one possibility. And a big reason we know that is because other people with Parkinson's-- the confronters, not the deniers-- are talking about it.
<v Nancy Updike>帕金森病与阿尔茨海默病不同。认知问题因人而异，而且仍在研究中。但痴呆是一种可能性。我们知道这一点的一个重要原因是因为其他帕金森病人——那些对抗者，而不是否认者——正在谈论它。

00:53:43.420 --> 00:53:55.500
<v Michael  Kinsley>Most of our information about cognitive problems comes from the patient, him or herself. And I'm thinking, are these people out of their minds?
<v Michael  Kinsley>我们关于认知问题的大部分信息来自病人他自己。我在想，这些人失去理智了吗？

00:53:55.500 --> 00:54:03.480
<v Nancy Updike>You want to send a mass email to everybody with Parkinson's just saying, can we all just not talk about this? Why are we talking about this?
<v Nancy Updike>你想给每个患有帕金森病的人发一封群邮件，只是说，我们能都不谈论这个吗？为什么我们在谈论这个？

00:54:03.480 --> 00:54:09.220
<v Michael  Kinsley>Yes. Well, when I hear people talking about this, I think, shut up. Shut up.
<v Michael  Kinsley>是的。嗯，当我听到人们谈论这个时，我想，闭嘴。闭嘴。

00:54:09.220 --> 00:54:11.790
<v Nancy Updike>You actually think "shut up" at them?
<v Nancy Updike>你实际上在想“闭嘴”对他们说？

00:54:11.790 --> 00:54:32.820
<v Michael  Kinsley>Yeah. It doesn't work. I switch back into denial when it comes to the cognitive thing. And yet here I am, discussing it on a national radio show, so obviously my motives are mixed.
<v Michael  Kinsley>是的。没用。当涉及到认知问题时，我切换回否认状态。然而我却在这里，在全国电台节目中讨论它，所以显然我的动机是混合的。

00:54:32.820 --> 00:54:35.250
<v Nancy Updike>Do you not want to talk about it?
<v Nancy Updike>你不想谈论它吗？

00:54:35.250 --> 00:54:40.340
<v Michael  Kinsley>Well, we have nothing else to talk about, Nancy.
<v Michael  Kinsley>嗯，我们没别的话可谈了，南希。

00:54:40.340 --> 00:54:41.920
<v Nancy Updike>I'm sure we could find something.
<v Nancy Updike>我确信我们能找到什么。

00:54:41.920 --> 00:54:44.820
<v Michael  Kinsley>Yes, well, we've got the New Hampshire primary.
<v Michael  Kinsley>是的，嗯，我们有新罕布什尔州初选。

00:54:44.820 --> 00:54:47.810
<v Nancy Updike>Yes, yes. Did you watch the Super Bowl?
<v Nancy Updike>是的，是的。你看超级碗了吗？

00:54:47.810 --> 00:54:48.540
<v Michael  Kinsley>No.
<v Michael  Kinsley>没有。

00:54:48.540 --> 00:54:50.840
<v Nancy Updike>No? Beyonce? Anything?
<v Nancy Updike>没有？碧昂丝？什么东西？

00:54:50.840 --> 00:54:51.380
<v Michael  Kinsley>Eh.
<v Michael  Kinsley>呃。

00:54:51.380 --> 00:54:52.070
<v Nancy Updike>No?
<v Nancy Updike>没有？

00:54:52.070 --> 00:54:59.410
<v Michael  Kinsley>No. What's she done?
<v Michael  Kinsley>没有。她做了什么？

00:54:59.410 --> 00:55:12.430
<v Ira Glass>Nancy Updike is one of the producers of our program. Michael Kinsley is a contributing columnist for Vanity Fair and The Washington Post . His latest book is Old Age: A Beginner's Guide .
